  "analytic_review_groups": [
    {
      "topic": "Hopf setup",
      "review": "n>=2, elliptic fiber mass two, probability form half alpha^(n-1) wedge zeta, and normalized Fubini--Study volume one."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Potential descent",
      "review": "Connected compact fibers and the pulled-back closed background meet the earlier submersion descent proposition, including polar fibers."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Section identification",
      "review": "Corresponding homogeneous polynomials and Fubini--Study pointwise norms agree for integer k>=0."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Weighted norm",
      "review": "The fiber factor two cancels the half in dV; nonnegative Borel integration applies to possibly infinite weighted norms and ignores smooth-volume-null pole sets."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Kernels and bounded algebra",
      "review": "The isometry preserves orthonormal bases and kernels; boundedness is equivalent under a surjective pullback, compatibly with products."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Measure definition",
      "review": "The fiber-integration formula defines pi^*nu wedge zeta on nonnegative Borel functions; its smooth-density case agrees with forms."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Curvature converse",
      "review": "Wedge the positive pulled-back-background current with semipositive zeta and push forward; zeta^2=0 and fiber mass two recover positivity downstairs."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Bounded top power",
      "review": "Bounded submersion products and base dimension n-1 leave the single term n pi^*(sigma+ddc v)^(n-1) wedge zeta."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Extended limit",
      "review": "Semipositive sigma permits bounded truncations; the level indicators pull back exactly and nonnegative fiber integration commutes with their increasing measures."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Mass and closed-class degeneracy",
      "review": "Closed base volume bounds arbitrary extended mass by one and equals one for bounded v; rank n-1 pulled-back closed currents have zero n-dimensional mass, including k=0."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Fiber variation",
      "review": "Every fiber-constant omega-potential descends and has mass at most 2n; unbounded threefold masses therefore require fiber variation."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Equation constants",
      "review": "Pushing forward gives 2n times the base equation; bounded Stokes normalization forces c=2n/integral f and the density is normalized correctly."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Bounded uniqueness scope",
      "review": "The existing Guan--Li theorem gives uniqueness up to constants among bounded potentials; the final descended uniqueness sentence now retains that bound. Smooth positive density uses the earlier smooth existence input."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Parameter equation",
      "review": "The exponential right-hand side transforms with the same c/(2n) factor without asserting extra uniqueness for arbitrary lambda."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Torus invariance",
      "review": "The individual coordinate-rotation action is stated; complex-scalar invariance gives the common logarithmic translation and gradient sum k."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Hessian and simplex",
      "review": "The complex matrix is diagonally congruent to the real Hessian. Bounded base potential and n>=2 force every coordinate derivative nonnegative; transverse strict positivity forces it positive."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Gradient bijection",
      "review": "On the zero-sum hyperplane, k max x-p dot x is positive homogeneous and has positive sphere minimum for interior p; coercivity supplies the unique minimizer, the coordinate-sum identity removes the multiplier, and strict convexity supplies injectivity."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Editorial and preservation",
      "review": "Both spacing guards were checked in the actual PDF, all three local and all 1233 global proof bodies are exact, and no mathematical statement environment changed."
    }
  ],
